---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SimpleFooter from "../components/SimpleFooter.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="openEHR Postman Alternative — Tool Comparison | openEHR Explorer"
  description="Looking for a Postman alternative for openEHR? Compare openEHR Explorer against Postman/curl, openEHRTool v2, Better Studio, and ehr-ctrl feature by feature."
  keywords="openEHR Postman, Postman alternative for openEHR, openEHR Postman collection, openEHR REST API testing, EHRBase Postman"
  path="compare.html"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
      {
        "@context": "https://schema.org",
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Is there an official Postman collection for openEHR?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Not a universal one. Individual CDR vendors publish their own OpenAPI specs and sometimes example Postman collections, but there's no single canonical openEHR Postman collection — every CDR has slightly different endpoints, and Postman has no built-in concept of templates, FLAT paths, or archetype node IDs. openEHR Explorer replaces the collection-building step entirely: point it at a server and it discovers EHRs, templates, and compositions for you."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Can I use Postman to test openEHR REST APIs?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "Yes — Postman (or curl) works fine for raw openEHR REST calls against EHRBase, Better Platform, or any other openEHR CDR, since they're standard HTTP/JSON APIs. What Postman can't do is resolve archetype node IDs to readable labels, render a Web Template as a tree, or autocomplete FLAT/AQL paths — that's the gap openEHR Explorer fills."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "What's a good Postman alternative for openEHR development?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "openEHR Explorer is a free, open-source desktop alternative to Postman built specifically for openEHR: it understands EHR status, compositions, templates, and AQL natively, so you don't hand-build requests or decode responses by hand."
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Does openEHR Explorer replace Postman entirely?",
            "acceptedAnswer": {
              "@type": "Answer",
              "text": "No — Postman is still a solid general-purpose REST client. openEHR Explorer is a focused tool for the openEHR-specific parts of the workflow: browsing EHRs, inspecting templates, building compositions, and running AQL. Many developers keep both installed and reach for whichever fits the task."
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    </script>
  </Fragment>

  <SiteHeader current="compare" />

  <main class="container">
    <div class="page-hero">
      <h1>The Postman for openEHR</h1>
      <p>
        A Postman alternative built specifically for openEHR, and how it stacks up against every other developer tool in the ecosystem. No
        dedicated openEHR GUI existed &mdash; until now.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="summary" style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 56px;">
      <h2>Why not just use Postman for openEHR?</h2>
      <div class="summary-grid">
        <div class="summary-item">
          <strong>No openEHR Postman collection ships by default</strong>
          <p>Postman has no notion of EHRs, compositions, or templates &mdash; every request against an openEHR CDR is built and read by hand.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="summary-item">
          <strong>Node IDs stay raw</strong>
          <p>Postman shows at0006, not &ldquo;Systolic&rdquo;. You still need the OPT or Web Template open in another tab to decode a response.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="summary-item">
          <strong>No FLAT path autocomplete</strong>
          <p>Building a FLAT composition body in Postman means hand-typing every archetype path from the template JSON.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="summary-item">
          <strong>Postman is still great for raw HTTP</strong>
          <p>
            openEHR Explorer isn't trying to replace Postman for general API work &mdash; it's what to reach for once you're specifically working
            with openEHR CDR data. See the <a href="#comparison">full feature comparison</a> below.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

